92186124724 #edb/ref 1050 900.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124724 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "2f9b26dc-7c78-5bb7-9eb0-e6029679e710" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125288 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125288 #edb/ref 1061 11087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125288 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a0c271d5-ca3c-5651-a78d-a2b18242c45c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125288 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125289 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125289 #edb/ref 1061 11090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125289 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9e271cad-793b-51f0-9124-47a943c42f3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125289 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125290 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125290 #edb/ref 1061 11092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125290 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "98229d4f-e216-5858-8b87-e9442dce8ede" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125290 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125291 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125291 #edb/ref 1061 11094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125291 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a9b09430-2391-566d-ac41-6360e135665e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125291 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125297 #edb/ref 1061 11197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125297 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b6bdeefa-e28b-5e72-8c05-e9f8fb9c7542" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125297 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125329 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125329 #edb/ref 1061 15456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125329 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6285e0db-558c-5e11-85ab-0b82dc834963" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125329 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125341 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125341 #edb/ref 1061 17452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125341 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f746dfa7-8c4b-5575-86b0-34ab4d9d09e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125341 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125342 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125342 #edb/ref 1061 17454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125342 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "22604921-be1a-5e8f-bdd7-8ac9e59c63a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125342 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125466 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125466 #edb/ref 1061 23425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125466 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "af7ce556-917b-54ac-a772-adecff1e36f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125466 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125467 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125467 #edb/ref 1061 23498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125467 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "29991074-71d2-5a37-a971-5f658f37d3ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125467 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125521 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125521 #edb/ref 1061 30785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125521 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7b0d0289-9feb-5b9b-9cdf-8e8bea357afd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125521 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125522 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125522 #edb/ref 1061 30789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125522 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "605b2c91-28e4-589e-9ea6-93545635ab1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125522 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125523 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125523 #edb/ref 1061 30790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125523 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "befa8020-1248-59aa-b092-5fd7f5b45c2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125523 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125590 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125590 #edb/ref 1061 37506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125590 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "78f1d4af-7528-5705-8b7b-224b627efd47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125590 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125600 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125600 #edb/ref 1061 37838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125600 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1402186a-b64d-5406-9767-99815ff7ecc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125600 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125601 #edb/ref 1061 37839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125601 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "be30c408-bfbc-549b-b14c-3f8724059c1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125601 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125731 #edb/ref 1064 "We can extend our knowledge of limits to multivariable functions. We'll first consider the two-variable case. \n\nWe denote $\\mathbf{x}=(x,y)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_0 = (a,b)$ as a particular, fixed vector. Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined in some neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0.$ \n\nThen, the notation\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to \\mathbf{x}_0} f (x,y) = L\n$$\n\nmeans that the value of $f$ can be made as close as we want to $L,$ provided that $\\mathbf x$ is sufficiently close to $\\mathbf{x}_0.$\n\nA few things to note:\n\n- This definition of a limit also extends to functions of more than two variables in the natural way.\n\n- All of the rules set out by the algebra of limits are valid for functions of several variables.\n\nWe say that $f(x,y)$ is **continuous** at $(a,b)$ if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to (a,b)} f (x,y) = f(a,b).\n$$\n\nSo, if a function is continuous at $(a,b),$ then the limit can be evaluated by simply plugging $(x,y) = (a,b)$ into the function.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{xy}{x^2+y^2}\n$$\n\nThis function is continuous for all $(x,y)$ with the exception of $(0,0)$ where the denominator vanishes. Since the point $(2,1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(x,y) = (2,1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125731 #edb/ref 1065 2480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125731 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "eccfdda1-288a-5169-adde-a00163fa348f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125731 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125732 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that for a function of a single variable $f(x),$ if the left and right limits both exist and are equal at some point $x=a$, then this is sufficient to conclude that the limit exists at $x=a.$ \n\nSo, if we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x\\to a^+} f(x) = L,\n$$\n\nthen we may conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a} f(x) = L.\n$$\n\nFor a multivariable function, the situation is not so straightforward. For a function of two variables $f(x,y),$ we may only conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x} \\to \\mathbf{x}_0} f(x,y) = L,\n$$\n\nif we can show that the limit is the same *for all possible paths* on which $\\mathbf x$ can approach $\\mathbf{x}_0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125732 #edb/ref 1065 2482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125732 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b4b46a0a-5b1c-58eb-aa32-dd0ccd57f7a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125732 #edb/ref 1067 "The Definition of a Limit: Single Variable Functions vs. Multivariable Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125754 #edb/ref 1064 "Sometimes, we may need to simplify a limit before we can evaluate it. \n\nFor example, let's consider the limit\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}} \\dfrac{x^2+2xy}{xy+2y^2}.\n$$\n\nDirect substitution causes this limit to take the indeterminate form $\\dfrac 00$ because the numerator and denominator both evaluate to zero at the given point. \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}} \\dfrac{x^2+2xy}{xy+2y^2} = \\dfrac{(2)^2 + 2(2)(-1)}{(2)(-1) + 2(-1)^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by factoring the numerator and denominator and then canceling a common factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy}{xy+2{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{x(x+2y)}{y(x+2y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{x}{y}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}} \\dfrac{x^2+2xy}{xy+2y^2} = -2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125754 #edb/ref 1065 2883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125754 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8f5c0d1f-e38c-5cd7-97e2-1aaaac5158b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125754 #edb/ref 1067 "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Rational Functions by Factoring" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125759 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now compare some properties of addition in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$ \nIt turns out that the addition of two matrices in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is *very* similar to the addition of two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ See the table below.\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\" width=\"700\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30\"><b></b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"200\"><b>$\\mathbb{R}^2$</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"200\"><b>$\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Addition is Commutative:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A1</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any matrices $A, B \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}),$ we have $\\color{blue}A+B = B+A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Addition is Associative:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A2</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v})+\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{u}+(\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{w})$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any matrices $A, B, C \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}),$ we have $\\color{blue}(A+B) + C = A + (B+C)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Existence of a Unique Zero Element:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A3</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For the zero-vector $\\mathbf{0}$ and any $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For the zero-matrix $O$ and any $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}),$ we have $\\color{blue} A + O = A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Existence of a Unique Opposite Element:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A4</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ we can find the corresponding negative vector $-\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + (-\\mathbf{u}) = \\mathbf{0}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ such that $\\color{blue} A + (-A) = O$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nAnd it's not just matrices that have additive properties similar to those of $\\mathbb R^n.$ Many mathematical structures do. Let's see some more examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125759 #edb/ref 1065 3184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125759 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e21e1a72-75b8-5ae3-9b7d-96f08d2ffa86" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125759 #edb/ref 1067 "Addition of Vectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125809 #edb/ref 1064 "However, it is often easy to show that a limit does not exist for a multivariable function. All we have to do is find two paths on which the limit evaluates to different results. \n\nAs an example, let's consider the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)} \\dfrac{y}{x+y}\n$$\n\nClearly, direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00.$ Let's compute the limit along the paths $y=x$ and $y=x^2.$ We will see that the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{y}{x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{x+x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{1+x} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{y}{x +y} = \\text{DNE}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125809 #edb/ref 1065 3960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125809 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8040690e-6d4c-50ac-8b9f-f92615717816" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125809 #edb/ref 1067 "Proving That a Limit Does Not Exist by Evaluating It Along Different Paths" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125832 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's also compare some properties of scalar multiplication in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$ It turns out that the multiplication of a matrix in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ by a scalar is *very* similar to the multiplication of a vector in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ by a real number. See the table below.\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\" width=\"700\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30\"><b></b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"200\"><b>$\\mathbb{R}^2$</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"200\"><b>$\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Invariance Under Multiplication by $1$:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A5</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} 1 \\cdot A = A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Scalar Multiplication is Associative:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A6</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{u}) = (\\alpha\\beta) \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\beta A) = (\\alpha\\beta) A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Distributive Laws:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A7</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} (\\alpha + \\beta)\\mathbf{u} = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\beta \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} (\\alpha + \\beta)A = \\alpha A + \\beta A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A8</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any number $\\alpha$ and any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}) = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\alpha \\mathbf{v}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any number $\\alpha$ and any matrices $A, B \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (A + B) = \\alpha A + \\alpha B$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125832 #edb/ref 1065 4934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125832 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "05cdf1b2-a823-5410-8df1-42fa4da19240" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125832 #edb/ref 1067 "Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125833 #edb/ref 1064 "The structure $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is called a **vector space**. However, many other structures possess properties similar to $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ These other structures can be called vector spaces too. \n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn about some important properties used to construct vector spaces. We begin by discussing what it means for a set to be **closed** under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nAs an example, consider the set $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2 \\times 2$ matrices with real entries:\n\n![](images/a5/a53ec7e9416ae5eee4a1408df358302567a5aa98a393d6ddcb19e861645d2317.svg)\n\nFor this set of matrices, we can define two operations:\n\n- the addition of two matrices, and\n\n- the multiplication of a matrix by a real number (called a scalar).\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is **closed under addition**. This means that the sum of *any* two matrices from $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is also a matrix in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$\n\n- $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is **closed under scalar multiplication**. This means that the product of *any* scalar and *any* matrix from $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is also a matrix in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$\n\nIdentifying that a set is closed under addition and scalar multiplication is an essential first step in determining whether a set can form a vector space." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125833 #edb/ref 1065 4935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125833 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "18653f19-b4cf-5f0f-ab39-b1ae33ecdc6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125833 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125860 #edb/ref 1064 "It's important to realize that not all sets are closed under addition and scalar multiplication. Sets not closed under addition and scalar multiplication *cannot* be vector spaces. \n\nFor instance, let $S$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients whose degree is greater than or equal to $5.$  \n\nWe note that $S$ is not closed under addition. To see why this is the case, let's define the polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in S$ as \n\n$$\np(x) = x^5, \\qquad q(x) = 1-x^5.\n$$\n\nAdding the two polynomials using regular polynomial addition, we get\n\n$$\np(x) + q(x) = x^5 + (1-x^5) = 1\\notin S\n$$\n\nThe sum $p(x) + q(x)$ is not in $S$ because it is a polynomial of degree zero, which is less than $5.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that our set $S$ is *not* closed under addition." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125860 #edb/ref 1065 6230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125860 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b014039e-61b6-58d7-8f35-a72e6153c126" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125860 #edb/ref 1067 "Sets That Are Not Closed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125861 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we discussed several properties exhibited by vectors in $\\mathbb R^n$ and other sets.\nLet's consolidate these properties into a single list.\n\n| **Label** | **Name** | **Description** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| A1 | **Addition is Commutative** | For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A2 | **Addition is Associative** | For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v})+\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{u}+(\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{w})$ |\n| A3 | **Existence of a Unique Zero Element** | For the zero-vector $\\mathbf{0}$ and any $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A4 | **Existence of a Unique Opposite Element** | For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ we can find the corresponding negative vector $-\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + (-\\mathbf{u}) = \\mathbf{0}$ |\n| A5 | **Invariance Under Multiplication by $1$** | For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A6 | **Scalar Multiplication is Associative** | For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{u}) = (\\alpha\\beta) \\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A7 | **Distributive Laws:** | For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} (\\alpha + \\beta)\\mathbf{u} = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\beta \\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A8 | **Distributive Laws:** | For any number $\\alpha$ and any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}) = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\alpha \\mathbf{v}$ |\n\nWe'll meet these properties again in a future lesson, where we'll formally define what we mean by a vector space." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125861 #edb/ref 1065 6231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125861 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "72e9ea87-7b8e-5d00-821a-31813b203a2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125861 #edb/ref 1067 "A Summary of the Addition and Scalar Multiplication Properties of Euclidian Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128515 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186128516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128515 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186128517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128515 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186128518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128515 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186128519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128515 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186128520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128515 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128515 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128515 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186128517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128515 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "73f20238-5852-5de2-b7d7-1ef3dacb6c32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128516 #edb/ref 1080 "images/e3/e3e87ba472c52ffd7f6df89f4ad9886e25a3d64cd076d90a085adb38ce6c1508.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128516 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128516 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "eb1638ba-1d0a-5da1-b7e4-2f08f45f829e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128517 #edb/ref 1080 "images/28/28302f0f3d075a9a0b698613b479cf777c00ece0be6766de79c5ced5e01ff63d.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128517 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128517 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "310b1f99-2ddc-5ff7-abaa-c6dcc52e7c0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128518 #edb/ref 1080 "None of the listed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128518 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128518 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "31a38edb-3150-59ed-b9a2-5ca4530919a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128519 #edb/ref 1080 "images/7c/7c8b602894c58ee03f2e0cc3be9155d177da9915f537482671609de075a6bc80.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128519 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128519 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "38a2fee1-773a-5688-8132-96509ebe923c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128520 #edb/ref 1080 "images/73/73fce52068582bba934a9db9b3ccd130b7022a752eb3e753aa7b1a1eb1eab40f.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128520 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128520 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "40974dcc-78f6-5b35-bca7-a00d9c372e85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129167 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129167 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129167 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129167 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129167 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129167 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129167 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129167 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129167 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129167 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129167 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129167 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129167 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186429974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129167 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129167 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Type I Regions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129167 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129167 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "64d342be-ebaf-5e94-b641-ea8ce6f14c12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129168 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129168 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129168 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129168 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129168 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129168 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129168 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129168 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129168 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129168 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129168 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186429975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129168 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129168 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Type II Regions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129168 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129168 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "85335de6-e1dc-5a2f-b30a-806627759030" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186406982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1039 "Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7948909f-8dcb-5cc0-a00f-f505a41e4004" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129327 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129327 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129327 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129327 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129327 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129327 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129327 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129327 #edb/ref 1045 "Type I and II Regions in Two-Dimensional Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129327 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186130023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129327 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129327 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129327 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "c092012f-6280-5531-b39f-09c65d20ea5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129839 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129839 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71823" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129839 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129839 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is a type II region in two-dimensional space?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129839 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5eea10eb-d5b3-52da-8f82-0b23089e27dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129839 #edb/ref 1074 "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nA type II region has the property that a horizontal line drawn through the region intersects the region in a single interval only.\n\nAmong the given options, the only type II region is the following.\n\n![](images/3f/3fccfbd6fd95005958fc9c9c14a1d327c27ef21351b1a32f3a5da553fdbf1539.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130023 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130023 #edb/ref 1061 22589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130023 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0b518252-fe1c-57d7-bc6f-6a00d3addac9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130023 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130024 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130024 #edb/ref 1061 22590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130024 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1b6899de-a336-56a9-809a-31cc01aeebb8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130024 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130025 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130025 #edb/ref 1061 22591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130025 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4858561d-380d-50d9-901d-e33ce219f2d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130025 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130026 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130026 #edb/ref 1061 22592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130026 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "952b663b-02bc-55c3-b391-c854df6de6fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130026 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130027 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130027 #edb/ref 1061 22593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130027 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eafe2c8b-e7b1-59e6-9468-2c731ee86e98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130027 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130028 #edb/ref 1061 22594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130028 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3b78b691-c694-59d1-b1ef-5ea8a77ad679" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130028 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130371 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the region that lies between the curves ${\\color{red}y=f(x)}$ and ${\\color{blue}y=g(x)}$ over a closed interval $[a,b]$ on the $x$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](images/a4/a45abf7904382a16daaa38ff617a9487b5c274cf65ef44f89e7feb0c32c496e0.png)\n\nThis region is an example of a **type I region**. Generally, a type I region is any region bounded by two vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b,$ and two curves $y=g(x)$ and $y=f(x),$ where $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions. \n\nUsing set notation, a type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: a \\leq x  \\leq b, \\quad g(x) \\leq y \\leq f(x) \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nIn a type I region, any vertical line drawn through the region should intersect the region in a *single* interval, as shown below.\n\n![](images/f7/f7282efbe2c9244c77ce6bbbeca047ee3ab29dd2fc90e0d2440c519328cb5973.png)\n\nBy contrast, the following region is *not* a type I region since there is a vertical line whose intersection with the region results in two *separate* line segments. In other words, the vertical line enters and leaves the region more than once.\n\n![](images/a2/a26c7588e297a8af35f1e709210596a94f3fb4bd84e8237dc1462f48b961630e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130371 #edb/ref 1065 3854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130371 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2658cc5e-d169-5ff8-871c-4ccaab428eb7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130371 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130372 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the region that lies between the curves ${\\color{red}x=f(y)}$ and ${\\color{blue}x=g(y)}$ over a closed interval $[a,b]$ on the $y$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](images/cf/cf2a2a41d63f21b279215fd1aa8d7361eb638e831b970bb3de861673f67612e6.png)\n\nThis region is an example of a **type II region**. In general, a type II region is any region that is bounded by two horizontal lines $y=a$ and $y=b,$ and two curves $x=g(y)$ and $x=f(y),$ where $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nUsing set notation, a type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: a \\leq y  \\leq b, \\quad g(y) \\leq x \\leq f(y) \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nIn a type II region, any horizontal line drawn through the region should intersect the region in a *single* interval, as shown below.\n\n![](images/56/56019e8cc2b1146b99fa6f6532356281fdb847c342c25c63f843ddee4f8cb197.png)\n\nBy contrast, the following region is *not* a type II region since there is a horizontal line whose intersection with the region results in two *separate* line segments. In other words, the horizontal line enters and leaves the region more than once.\n\n![](images/a7/a7c1ead1274250f7184b132eff58a3028ce648715dcbfa7c7046077fd4e680ef.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130372 #edb/ref 1065 3855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130372 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d089f833-c09a-5229-9be0-8cffa2510d60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130372 #edb/ref 1067 "Type II Regions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130373 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, a given region can be type I, type II, both, or neither.\n\nFor example, let's consider the region in the coordinate plane bounded between the circle ${\\color{blue}x^2+y^2=2}$ and the lines ${\\color{red}x=-1},$ ${\\color{red}x=1},$ ${\\color{red}y=-1}$, and ${\\color{red}y=1},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/db/db43502c2b096c912ac38d297c3a06e7dcbf4945af6fbc7f5e365a40d11fcde8.png)\n\nThere is a vertical line (for instance, the $y$-axis) whose intersection with the region results in two separate line segments. In other words, the vertical line enters and leaves the region more than once. So, this region *cannot* be a type I region.\n\n![](images/dc/dc1dca3b3015da15f554d95dae7add65aeee0b83a0075ec5295716a3e86d71f5.png)\n\nSimilarly, there is a horizontal line (for instance, the $x$-axis) whose intersection with the region results in two separate line segments. In other words, the horizontal line enters and leaves the region more than once. So, this region *cannot* be a type II region.\n\n![](images/82/82720f4b69fc3e63bbf54cb22d60299035faf91a6021f09d41e9f62dad571581.png)\n\nIn conclusion, this region is *neither* type I nor type II.\n\nLet's now look at an example that is *both* type I and type II." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130373 #edb/ref 1065 3856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130373 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "09369aae-8943-5475-9739-20e6619b16d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130373 #edb/ref 1067 "Regions that are Neither Type I Nor Type II" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186266040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186266041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186367352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186368764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404058 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152169 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Solutions to Linear ODEs Using the Superposition Principle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152169 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152169 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152192 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152192 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152192 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152192 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152192 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186266034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152192 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186266035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152192 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186266036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152192 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186266037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152192 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152192 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152192 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152192 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152192 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152192 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186266038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186266039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186367338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186367345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152193 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152193 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152193 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152307 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152307 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152307 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152307 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152307 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152307 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152307 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152307 #edb/ref 1045 "The Superposition Principle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152307 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186153088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152307 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152307 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186152307 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "de6336c1-f5b4-5304-9fdd-384cbc487c0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153075 #edb/ref 1061 24210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153075 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "230b3a14-dd22-5c8a-94c5-5be6dc2128a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153075 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153088 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153088 #edb/ref 1061 24527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153088 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a1ef948a-d0ad-5a62-bffa-6701e75912a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153088 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153109 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153109 #edb/ref 1061 25425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153109 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f01e7386-b41a-5337-b62d-62077f6bdb02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153109 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153110 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153110 #edb/ref 1061 25426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153110 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b8ccaf97-01ff-5727-8fc0-3612aa7915d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153110 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153115 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153115 #edb/ref 1061 25485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153115 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b6ebe766-97a0-541f-b401-9aec830d1c73" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153115 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153116 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153116 #edb/ref 1061 25486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153116 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d88e8300-d051-5777-b95d-2efea037150a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153116 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153364 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that a second-order linear ODE can always be expressed in the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x).\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that the equation is *homogeneous* if $f(x) = 0$ for all $x{:}$\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y =0\n$$\n\nThe **superposition principle** states that if the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are solutions to a linear homogeneous differential equation, then the function \n\n$$\ny(x) = y_1(x) + y_2(x)\n$$\n\nis also a solution to the equation.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the following homogeneous differential equation:\n\n$$\ny'' + 16y = 0\n$$\n\n \n\nTwo solutions of this equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos 4x, \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin 4x.\n$$\n\nWe can easily check that these are solutions by substituting them into the equation and verifying that they result in true statements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{''}+16{y}_{1} & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}4x)^{''}+16\\operatorname{cos}4x & =0 \\\\ (-4\\operatorname{sin}4x)^{'}+16\\operatorname{cos}4x & =0 \\\\ -16\\operatorname{cos}4x+16\\operatorname{cos}4x & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {y}_{2}^{''}+16{y}_{2} & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{sin}4x)^{''}+16\\operatorname{sin}4x & =0 \\\\ (4\\operatorname{cos}4x)^{'}+16\\operatorname{sin}4x & =0 \\\\ -16\\operatorname{sin}4x+16\\operatorname{sin}4x & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the equation is linear and homogeneous, the superposition principle guarantees that the sum\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}4x+\\operatorname{sin}4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis also a solution to the differential equation. \n\nWe can check that this is a solution to the equation by substituting (keeping the order consistent with our definition):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{''}+16y & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}4x+\\operatorname{sin}4x)^{''}+16(\\operatorname{cos}4x+\\operatorname{sin}4x) & =0 \\\\ (-4\\operatorname{sin}4x+4\\operatorname{cos}4x)^{'}+16(\\operatorname{cos}4x+\\operatorname{sin}4x) & =0 \\\\ (-16\\operatorname{cos}4x-16\\operatorname{sin}4x)+16\\operatorname{cos}4x+16\\operatorname{sin}4x & =0 \\\\ 0=0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The superposition principle does *not* extend to the products or quotients of the known solutions. For example, the functions\n\n$$\n\\cos4x\\sin4x, \\quad \\sin^24x, \\quad \\dfrac{\\sin4x}{\\cos 4x},\n$$\n\nare *not* solutions to our original differential equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153364 #edb/ref 1065 4087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153364 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c370ce9c-1004-553d-8faf-cd8c882feea2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153364 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153383 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous ODE\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = 0.\n$$\n\nThe superposition principle also states that if $y_1(x)$ is a solution to the equation, then the function \n\n$$\ny(x) = cy_1(x)\n$$\n\nis also a solution to the equation, where $c$ is a constant. \n\nTo illustrate, let's once again consider the following linear differential equation:\n\n$$\ny'' + 16y = 0\n$$\n\n \n\nRecall that\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos 4x, \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin 4x\n$$\n\n are solutions to this equation. So, according to the superposition principle, the functions\n\n$$\n6\\cos 4x, \\quad -\\sin 4x, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\pi}{e}\\cos 4x\n$$\n\nare all solutions to the ODE.\n\nWe can check that these are indeed solutions to the equation by substituting. To illustrate, let's verify the solution $6\\cos 4x = 6y_1(x)$ by evaluating the left-hand side of this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{''}+16y & =(6\\operatorname{cos}4x)^{''}+16(6\\operatorname{cos}4x) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}4x)^{''}+6\\cdot 16(\\operatorname{cos}4x) \\\\  & =6\\cdot ((\\operatorname{cos}4x)^{''}+16\\operatorname{cos}4x) \\\\  & =6\\cdot ((-4\\operatorname{sin}4x)^{'}+16\\operatorname{cos}4x) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (-16\\operatorname{cos}4x+16\\operatorname{cos}4x) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** This idea extends to *scalar* (or constant) multiples only. For example, the function $x\\cos 4x$ is *not* a solution to our ODE." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153383 #edb/ref 1065 4198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153383 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3d8a4098-396f-59ef-bbc5-30f0f4342942" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153383 #edb/ref 1067 "Constant Multiples of Solutions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153384 #edb/ref 1064 "In its most general form, the **superposition principle** states that any linear combination (or \"superposition\") of solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation is also a solution. More precisely:\n\n> *If the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c_1$ and $c_2$ are constants, then $y(x) = c_1 y_1(x) + c_2 y_2(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.*\n\nNote that the superposition principle follows immediately from the definition of a linear differential operator $L.$ Recall that every homogeneous equation can be written as\n\n$$\nL(y) = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, let $y = c_1 y_1(x) + c_2y_2(x).$ Then, by the linearity of $L,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(y) & =L({c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x)) \\\\  & =L({c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x))+L({c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x)) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}L({y}_{1}(x))+{c}_{2}L({y}_{2}(x)) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\cdot 0+{c}_{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose we're told that the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' - y = 0\n$$\n\nhas two solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^x \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad y_2(x) = e^{-x}.\n$$\n\nThen, according to the superposition principle, any linear combination\n\n$$\ny(x) = c_1 e^x + c_2 e^{-x}\n$$\n\nis also a solution.\n\n- For instance, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =2{y}_{1}(x)-\\frac{1}{3}{y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis also a solution because it takes the form $c_1 e^x + c_2 e^{-x}$ with $c_1 = 2$ and $c_2 = -\\dfrac{1}{3}.$ $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- However, we *cannot* conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={x}^{2}{y}_{1}(x)+\\operatorname{sin}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{sin}(x){e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a solution because $x^2$ and $\\sin(x)$ are not constants. $\\quad \\color{red}\\times$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153384 #edb/ref 1065 4199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153384 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4e57a559-0bde-5f71-b99f-93c685361eb1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153384 #edb/ref 1067 "The Superposition Principle in Its Most General Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259907 #edb/ref 1080 "r\\cdot ⟨3,1,1⟩=12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259907 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259907 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0387b95b-ea4c-5624-9fdd-7d44aae31856" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260024 #edb/ref 1080 "\\sqrt{7}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260024 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260024 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1066db60-12af-5e82-a7c0-d24afa450e35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260107 #edb/ref 1080 "r\\cdot ⟨-4,-4,3⟩=11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260107 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260107 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1aa825cb-f5f2-5941-b18f-a35a9e8ffaab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260339 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260339 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260339 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "336d23d9-d848-54ca-86e6-782c1b13a369" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260385 #edb/ref 1080 "r\\cdot ⟨-4,3,4⟩=12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260385 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260385 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "394ffd1f-3104-5495-902a-37954445a0c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260617 #edb/ref 1080 "r\\cdot ⟨3,4,4⟩=23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260617 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260617 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "56cda873-6a65-51e4-b130-2eb9c208d8a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260760 #edb/ref 1080 "r\\cdot ⟨1,-2,4⟩=22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260760 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260760 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "65e4dacb-b64f-5663-ae54-844ec9e37c4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260820 #edb/ref 1080 "-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260820 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260820 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6bfd83ea-5bb5-57e7-b814-e0e365ce1b44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261298 #edb/ref 1080 "r\\cdot ⟨-2,2,1⟩